From 27eb878ad3acd7c338b540bd334c02528d015e1a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lovelace's Square <noreply@lovelacesquare.org>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 10:54:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] mcp: --automation-read-root and --automation-write-root
 confine the files an agent reaches

`gridcraft-cli mcp` let an agent open, load, save and export any file the
user can. As in WordCraft and DeckCraft, the headless server now takes
a read root and a write root, and every path a command reads or writes
must lie inside the matching one.

- engine: file_access (from WordCraft): AutomationRoots, checked by
  io::read_file (open, data.getData/fromTextCsv, insert.picture) and
  io::write_file (save, save as, PDF export and print to a file). Paths
  are made absolute and their links followed (for a missing file, its
  nearest existing folder's), then compared by whole components, without
  regard to case on Windows; `..` below a missing folder, dangling links
  and Windows device names are refused. A root left out grants no access
  of its kind. Roots apply per thread (confine) or to the whole process
  (confine_process). The spelling word lists, the system's own files,
  are read through io::read_system_text; a test keeps engine code from
  touching the file system outside io.
- mcp: Headless::with_automation_roots runs every call confined.
- cli: `mcp --automation-read-root DIR --automation-write-root DIR`
  (`--flag DIR` or `--flag=DIR`, once; folders must exist; a malformed
  flag is an error, never a root silently left out); confines the whole
  process and says so on stderr. `--in FILE` is opened before. With
  --connect the roots are refused: they don't confine a running app.
- docs: mcp.md, cli.md.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
